@copilot there is a fundamental error in your implementation when docker is involved. for repetition, the goal of this PR is to store duplicate versions only once in the big tar file (as the lowest version of the batch that is similar) and then extract that under the version that is being used. so for example, if node packages for node version 1, 2 and 3 are all equal, we only store 1 in the tar. if then node 3 is used, we extract 1 but call it 3 on disk and all is good. this is a perfect implementation for anything that is not docker.

if there is a docker on the system, we dont know what node version is used. this current implementation is then blindly extracting all versions. that is necessary and correct so far. However, if, in the above example, node 2 is used, only 1 is on disk, even though 1 would be valuable for 2. this implementation, if there is a docker or its a gihub actions (which means there is always a docker or node may be manually deployed) it doesnt list any node version, causing the entire tar to be unpacked. leading to exactly this behavior. i think instead of listing no node versions, we should list ALL node versions in such a case so that we forcefully unpack all versions and name them correctly on disk. we can probably easily get the lowest version by checking the contents of the tar and using the lowest. but the highest? where do we get that from? there are docker files in the instrumentation folder that we could use. they are only placeholders for renovate to indicate the latest. currently they are stripped during package build time. but we could not strip them, and read the max version from there.

all that i have written here is also valid and must be done for python.
